CS 214 Project III: A simple shell

Partners:
  Robert Orzol - rao73
  Danny Vinajero - dv328

Building and running:
  make
  ./mysh              interactive if stdin is a terminal, otherwise batch
  ./mysh script.sh    batch mode, reading commands from script.sh

Design Notes:
  Files:
    mysh.c     input loop, interactive/batch mode, prompt, status messages
    parse.c    tokenizer, comments, parser, wildcard expansion
    execute.c  path search, fork/execv, redirection, pipes, built-ins
    job.h      struct command / struct job shared by the parser and executor

  Input: one loop for both modes. read() fills a buffer; each complete line
  is run as soon as its newline is found, before read() is called again, so
  interactive mode never blocks while a full command is waiting. Lines can
  be any length (the line buffer grows with realloc). A final line without
  a newline is run at end of input.

  Modes: with a script argument mysh is always in batch mode; with no
  argument, isatty(STDIN_FILENO) decides. Only interactive mode prints the
  welcome/goodbye messages, the prompt, and "Exited with status N" /
  "Terminated by signal X" (via strsignal). In batch mode children get
  /dev/null as standard input, so a program cannot consume the rest of a
  piped-in script.

  Prompt: getcwd(), with the $HOME prefix replaced by ~ only when it is a
  whole path prefix (so /home/user2 is not shortened for HOME=/home/user).

  Parsing: tokens are split on whitespace; # starts a comment. A line is
  split on | into commands. <, > and >> take the next token as the file
  name. Syntax errors (missing file name, missing command around |, two
  input or two output redirections in one command) print an error and the
  line is skipped.

  Wildcards: a token containing * is split at its last / into a directory
  and a pattern. Names that begin with the text before * and end with the
  text after * match; patterns starting with * skip hidden files. Matches
  are added in sorted order; if none match, the token is passed unchanged.

  Execution: a name containing / is used as a path; otherwise /usr/local/bin,
  /usr/bin and /bin are searched with access(X_OK). Programs run with execv.
  Redirection uses open() + dup2() in the child; > and >> create files with
  mode 0640. A pipeline of n commands uses n-1 pipes; all unused pipe ends
  are closed, and the job succeeds if the last command succeeds.

  Built-ins: cd (no argument = $HOME), pwd, which, exit. A built-in alone
  runs in the shell; inside a pipeline it runs in a child. Any command line
  containing exit ends the shell after the whole line finishes.

  Signals: in interactive mode the shell ignores SIGINT, and each child
  restores the default before execv, so Ctrl+C stops the running program
  but not the shell.

Test Plan:
  Test scripts are in tests/ and run in batch mode with ./mysh tests/NAME.sh.
  Each targets one part of the spec:

  basic.sh            commands, comment-only lines, empty lines, trailing
                      comments
  redirect.sh         > creates/truncates, >> appends, < reads input
  pipes.sh            a 2-stage and a 3-stage pipeline
  builtins.sh         pwd; cd to a directory, to $HOME (no argument), to a
                      missing directory (error) and with too many
                      arguments (error); which for found and missing names
  syntax.sh           "< <", "| ls", "ls |", "echo >" all report a syntax
                      error and the shell continues with the next line
  status.sh           failing programs (false, unknown command) do not stop
                      the shell
  exit.sh             exit stops the shell; later lines are not run
  exit_pipe.sh        exit inside a pipeline: the pipeline finishes, then
                      the shell exits
  wildcard.sh         *.c, a pattern in a subdirectory (baz/foo*bar), and
                      an unmatched pattern passed through unchanged; the
                      files are in tests/globtest (includes a hidden file
                      that *.c must not match)
  builtin_redirect.sh pwd and which with output redirection
  paths.sh            running a program by full path; a bad path reports
                      an error
  batch_stdin.sh      run as "cat tests/batch_stdin.sh | ./mysh": cat gets
                      /dev/null, so it does not swallow the next line

  Other checks:
  - Files created by > and >> have mode 640 (checked with ls -l).
  - ./mysh nosuchfile prints an error and exits with status 1.
  - Interactive mode, by hand: welcome/goodbye messages, prompt with ~,
    prompt updates after cd, "Exited with status 1" after false,
    "Terminated by signal Interrupt" after Ctrl+C during sleep 30 (the
    shell keeps running), and typed-ahead input is not read until the
    running command finishes.
